1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,504
You now know how to specify the dataset, the rubric, and the threshold.

2
00:00:04,504 --> 00:00:09,359
All three answer some version of the same question: how often does this feature get

3
00:00:09,359 --> 00:00:09,886
it right.

4
00:00:09,886 --> 00:00:14,741
None of them answer a completely different question, one just as important: when it

5
00:00:14,741 --> 00:00:17,198
gets it wrong, what actually happens next.

6
00:00:17,198 --> 00:00:22,054
Does the wrong answer sail straight through to the user, stated as confidently as a

7
00:00:22,054 --> 00:00:23,575
right one would have been.

8
00:00:23,575 --> 00:00:24,920
Does anything catch it.

9
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:29,659
Is there a fallback, or is showing the raw output the only path the system knows.

10
00:00:29,659 --> 00:00:34,339
That's what a failure taxonomy and a degradation path are for, and almost no PRD

11
00:00:34,339 --> 00:00:35,567
specifies either one.

12
00:00:35,567 --> 00:00:39,428
Think about the last production incident you actually sat through.

13
00:00:39,428 --> 00:00:44,284
The retro almost never says "the pass rate was too low." It says something like "it

14
00:00:44,284 --> 00:00:49,081
told a customer their refund was approved when it wasn't, and nobody caught it for

15
00:00:49,081 --> 00:00:51,596
six hours." That's not a threshold problem.

16
00:00:51,596 --> 00:00:56,510
It's a missing answer to the question this lecture asks: what was supposed to happen

17
00:00:56,510 --> 00:01:00,839
the moment that specific kind of wrong answer occurred, and why didn't it?

18
00:01:01,389 --> 00:01:06,309
Here's why a single pass rate flattens exactly the distinction that matters most.

19
00:01:06,309 --> 00:01:10,075
All five of these count as one failure in a simple percentage.

20
00:01:10,075 --> 00:01:12,322
They are not remotely the same event.

21
00:01:12,322 --> 00:01:16,756
A confidently wrong answer is the dangerous one, the failure mode lecture

22
00:01:16,756 --> 00:01:21,554
one-point-five spent a whole hour on: nothing about how it looks tells you it's

23
00:01:21,554 --> 00:01:21,919
wrong.

24
00:01:21,919 --> 00:01:25,988
A timeout is annoying but honest, the system visibly didn't answer.

25
00:01:25,988 --> 00:01:30,847
An over-cautious refusal costs you a bit of usefulness and costs nobody anything

26
00:01:30,847 --> 00:01:31,151
real.

27
00:01:31,151 --> 00:01:36,192
Treating all five as interchangeable "failures" means your ninety percent pass rate

28
00:01:36,192 --> 00:01:41,233
could be hiding a small number of the first kind and a large number of the harmless

29
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:44,148
kind, and the number alone can't tell you which.

30
00:01:44,148 --> 00:01:49,250
Building this table is a five-minute exercise with a habit behind it, not a research

31
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:49,736
project.

32
00:01:49,736 --> 00:01:54,838
For your own feature, picture the worst thing it's actually done, or could plausibly

33
00:01:54,838 --> 00:01:57,025
do, and ask which row it belongs in.

34
00:01:57,025 --> 00:02:02,066
Most features turn out to have three or four realistic failure types, not the dozen

35
00:02:02,066 --> 00:02:03,098
you might expect.

36
00:02:03,098 --> 00:02:05,467
The value isn't the length of the list.

37
00:02:05,467 --> 00:02:10,448
It's that each row now has an owner and a response, instead of dissolving into one

38
00:02:10,448 --> 00:02:11,905
undifferentiated number.

39
00:02:12,455 --> 00:02:17,345
Here's the branch most PRDs never draw, because most PRDs only draw the top path.

40
00:02:17,345 --> 00:02:21,932
A request comes in, the model generates an answer, and then something has to

41
00:02:21,932 --> 00:02:26,943
actually check it before it reaches anyone: a confidence score, a guardrail, a rule

42
00:02:26,943 --> 00:02:29,478
that catches the shape of a known failure.

43
00:02:29,478 --> 00:02:30,444
Passes, show it.

44
00:02:30,444 --> 00:02:35,212
Doesn't, and the feature needs a second, deliberate path, not silence and not a

45
00:02:35,212 --> 00:02:37,023
guess dressed up as an answer.

46
00:02:37,023 --> 00:02:41,732
Degrading gracefully means picking one of three honest moves: say plainly that

47
00:02:41,732 --> 00:02:46,259
you're not sure, hand the case to a human, or decline the request outright.

48
00:02:46,259 --> 00:02:51,209
All three beat showing a wrong answer with the same confident tone as a right one.

49
00:02:51,209 --> 00:02:56,279
The guardrail layer from Section two is what makes this branch technically possible.

50
00:02:56,279 --> 00:03:00,625
A PRD that never specifies it is a feature with only the top path built.

51
00:03:00,625 --> 00:03:05,454
Which of the three you pick should depend on the archetype and blast radius from

52
00:03:05,454 --> 00:03:09,318
earlier in this section, not on whichever felt easiest to build.

53
00:03:09,318 --> 00:03:14,147
A drafting tool can usually just disclaim and let the human reviewing it decide.

54
00:03:14,147 --> 00:03:18,674
An agent about to take an action is a different conversation: escalating or

55
00:03:18,674 --> 00:03:23,624
declining is often the only responsible choice, because there's no draft step left

56
00:03:23,624 --> 00:03:24,891
to catch a bad guess.

57
00:03:25,441 --> 00:03:30,403
Say the honest caveat, because the fix here has its own failure mode, and it's an

58
00:03:30,403 --> 00:03:32,301
easy one to walk straight into.

59
00:03:32,301 --> 00:03:37,262
Set the guardrail too sensitive, and the feature spends half its time saying "I'm

60
00:03:37,262 --> 00:03:42,224
not sure" about things it actually knew, or routing easy cases to a human who now

61
00:03:42,224 --> 00:03:45,409
has more work than the feature was supposed to save.

62
00:03:45,409 --> 00:03:50,186
That's not safe, it's just failing in a direction that's harder to notice on a

63
00:03:50,186 --> 00:03:50,798
dashboard.

64
00:03:50,798 --> 00:03:55,760
The degradation rate belongs in the PRD too, with its own threshold, the same way

65
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:56,923
the pass rate does.

66
00:03:57,473 --> 00:04:02,231
That's the definition worth holding onto: degradation is a decision, made in

67
00:04:02,231 --> 00:04:07,489
advance, not an emergency improvisation made the day something breaks in production.

68
00:04:07,489 --> 00:04:08,929
The exercise is direct.

69
00:04:08,929 --> 00:04:13,061
Look for the sentence in your PRD that describes the unhappy path.

70
00:04:13,061 --> 00:04:18,069
Most PRDs don't have one, because most teams write the feature they're hoping to

71
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:21,324
ship, not the one they're actually going to operate.

72
00:04:21,874 --> 00:04:23,611
One thing before you move on.

73
00:04:23,611 --> 00:04:28,344
List at least three different ways your feature can fail, not just "wrong," and

74
00:04:28,344 --> 00:04:31,399
notice how differently you'd want each one handled.

75
00:04:31,399 --> 00:04:36,371
Then take the most dangerous one on that list and write down, in one sentence, what

76
00:04:36,371 --> 00:04:38,827
the system actually does when it happens.

77
00:04:38,827 --> 00:04:43,859
If you can't finish that sentence yet, that's this week's real work, not a detail to

78
00:04:43,859 --> 00:04:44,817
leave for later.

79
00:04:44,817 --> 00:04:49,730
Next lecture is where the degradation path from this one gets a name and a design:

80
00:04:49,730 --> 00:04:54,103
human-in-the-loop, and exactly where in the flow a person needs to stand.
